--TEST--
a `{page?}` left out of `Core\Router::url`'s `$params` is not refused, because its whole segment is dropped — ADR 0077 §§ 2 and 4
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The positive twin of the two refusals in
// `reject/a-route-link-refuses-an-unknown-name-and-an-uncovered-capture.nvst`:
// a capture left out of `$params` is a compile error for `{id}` and is *not*
// one for `{page?}`, because an optional capture's whole segment is dropped
// when nothing fills it (ADR 0077 § 2, ADR 0102 § 4).
class Posts {
    // § 4 (ADR 0102): an optional capture is last, and the parameter it binds
    // to has a default — which is what makes the absent case well-typed rather
    // than nullable by accident.
    // ADR 0096 § 1: the decision is a required sibling of every `#[Route]`, and
    // `Core\Audience::Public` is § 1a's case for a route that is genuinely open.
    #[Core\Route(path: "/posts/{page?}", method: Core\Http\Method::Get, name: "Posts::list")]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function list(uint $page = 1): string { return "page " . $page; }

    #[Core\Route(path: "/posts/{id}/edit", method: Core\Http\Method::Get, name: "Posts::edit")]
    #[Core\Access(allow: Core\Audience::Public)]
    public function edit(uint $id): string { return "edit " . $id; }
}

// The bound, asserted on both sides at one call site each: the same key absent
// and present, on the same route. A `url` that treated `{page?}` like `{id}`
// would not compile at all, and one that dropped a supplied value would print
// the first line twice.
echo "absent=", Core\Router::url("Posts::list", []), "\n";
echo "present=", Core\Router::url("Posts::list", ["page" => 3]), "\n";

// The other side of the same rule, on the route next to it: a *required*
// capture supplied is substituted rather than dropped, so "an absent segment
// disappears" is a property of `?` and not of the substitution.
echo "required=", Core\Router::url("Posts::edit", ["id" => 7]), "\n";
--EXPECT--
absent=/posts
present=/posts/3
required=/posts/7/edit
